  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "Useful as a minimal carrier with clear layer boundaries, but it leaves freshness, audience, geography, and session binding to unspecified higher-layer protocols, so it is a weak standalone answer to replay outside conditions.",
    "strengths": [
      "Explicit pCID dispatch and unsupported-pCID failures keep interpretation boundaries visible.",
      "Deterministic CBOR preserves exact bytes for hashing, signing, and store-forward across protocol evolution.",
      "Wrapper-based signatures let stronger conditional-release semantics be layered without changing the base envelope."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "The base envelope has no native audience, geography, epoch, session, or nonce binding.",
      "Replay detection is undefined at this layer; old valid references can be resent unless higher-layer state is checked.",
      "Opaque payloads and wrapper-selected signatures limit generic human/LLM auditability.",
      "The specimen is still draft and does not define a frozen anti-replay wrapper family."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Mallory can replay a once-valid content reference outside allowed conditions if Dave's handler omits freshness and condition checks.",
      "Exact-byte opaque forwarding can preserve and redistribute stale release artifacts.",
      "Different wrapper pCIDs may implement freshness and revocation inconsistently."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "Which wrapper or payload pCID carries audience, geography, expiry, and session binding?",
      "How does Dave verify geofencing or audience claims peer-locally without central authority?",
      "What durable mechanism distinguishes a historically valid signature from a currently authorized release?",
      "Should replay defense rely on signed wrappers, feed/state records, per-recipient capabilities, or a combination?"
    ],
